IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `gst-registration`

-- A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) -- worked cases from the seat of the sole trader or business
-- owner: ss 9-5, 9-20, 9-40, 9-70, 9-75, 11-5 to 11-30, 17-5, 23-5, 23-10, 23-15, 25-1, 27-5, 29-10(3), 29-70(2), 29-80,
-- 31-5, 31-8, 31-10, 144-5, 188-10 to 188-30, 195-1 "taxi travel".
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked against a
-- decided case, a GST ruling or the GST Regulations (not deposited). Fixtures are generated by scratchpad
-- gen_a-new-tax-system-goods-and-services-tax-act-1999_baseline.py; expected results were written by hand from the text.
--
-- The registration turnover threshold: s 23-15 gives "$50,000; or such higher amount as the regulations specify". The
-- regulations are not deposited; unless a case says otherwise, the higher amount is ASSUMED to be $75,000 ($150,000 for a
-- non-profit body), the figure both life scenarios state.
--
-- The lives (workbench scenarios/au/wa):
-- jake k043 (2028-10-20): first tax return as a sole trader, $31,000, riding an e-bike for a food-delivery platform with
-- an ABN (k040). His takings are ASSUMED to be $31,000 over the 12 months and the same over the next 12; nothing in the
-- scenario says he carries passengers.
-- daniel d049 (2032-10-01): "GST registration when turnover reaches $75,000" -- for Jarrah Legal Technologies Pty Ltd, the
-- company Daniel founded. Jarrah's current GST turnover is ASSUMED to reach exactly $75,000 on that day and its projected
-- turnover to be $90,000; it is ASSUMED to be unregistered until then. Its later sale, laptop and quarter are illustrations,
-- not facts of the scenario.

